var msSimItems = []edakit.Item{
	{KP: 1, Q: "A mixed-signal simulator couples an event-driven digital kernel with an analog solver. What does the analog solver do between digital events?",
		A: "Integrates the circuit equations with its own adaptive timestep", D: []string{"Waits until the next clock edge", "Evaluates Boolean equations", "Copies the last digital value"},
		Why: "The analog side solves differential-algebraic equations continuously; the digital side advances by events."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "In Verilog-AMS, what does a connect module do at a boundary between a logic net and an electrical net?",
		A: "Converts between the two: a digital-to-analog driver with rise time and resistance, or an analog-to-digital threshold detector", D: []string{"Removes the net from simulation", "Merges both sides into one logic net", "Runs a DC operating point at every step"},
		Why: "Connect modules define how logic values become voltages and how voltages become logic values."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "An analog-to-digital connect module uses thresholds of 0.3 V and 0.6 V with hysteresis. A slow input lingers at 0.45 V. What does the digital side see?",
		A: "It keeps its previous logic value until a threshold is crossed", D: []string{"It sees X immediately and forever", "It toggles every timestep", "It sees the real value 0.45"},
		Why: "With hysteresis, values between the thresholds hold the previous state; some connect modules flag X after a timeout."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "Why must the analog solver and digital kernel synchronize at an analog threshold crossing?",
		A: "The crossing time defines when the digital event happens, so the solver must locate it accurately and hand it to the event queue", D: []string{"Because digital time runs backwards", "Because the digital kernel solves the matrix", "To recompute the DC operating point"},
		Why: "Event timing errors become functional errors, so the solver backs up or shortens its step to find the crossing."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "A SerDes testbench with a full transistor-level PLL simulates one microsecond per hour. Which change gives the biggest speed-up for checking digital calibration logic?",
		A: "Replace the PLL with a behavioral or real-number model validated against the transistor version", D: []string{"Tighten the analog tolerances", "Run the digital part at gate level", "Add more Monte Carlo samples"},
		Why: "The analog solver dominates runtime. A validated model keeps the behaviour the logic depends on while removing the stiff transistor equations."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "An AMS run is slow because the analog partition takes tiny timesteps near every digital edge. What causes this?",
		A: "Fast edges injected through connect modules create sharp transients that the timestep control must resolve", D: []string{"The digital kernel is single-threaded", "Logic X values", "DC convergence aids"},
		Why: "Sharp inputs force small steps for local truncation error; slower connect-module rise times or modelling choices reduce the cost."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "Which part of a mixed-signal SoC is usually kept at transistor level in a top-level simulation?",
		A: "Only the analog blocks whose detailed behaviour is under test, with the rest modeled behaviorally or in RTL", D: []string{"Every block, to be safe", "Only the digital blocks", "None, top-level runs are always fully behavioral"},
		Why: "Top-level runs mix abstraction levels so that the block under scrutiny is accurate and the rest is fast."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "What problem does a real-number model avoid that an electrical behavioral model does not?",
		A: "It runs entirely in the event-driven kernel, so no matrix solve or Newton iteration is needed", D: []string{"It removes the need for any verification", "It models every parasitic", "It makes the digital kernel continuous"},
		Why: "Real-valued signals pass between processes as events; an electrical model still goes through the analog solver."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "A behavioral ADC model passes every mixed-signal regression, but silicon shows missing codes. What is the most likely verification gap?",
		A: "The model omitted non-idealities such as comparator offset or DAC mismatch that cause the failure", D: []string{"The digital simulator was too slow", "The model was too accurate", "Connect modules always hide missing codes"},
		Why: "A model verifies only what it represents. Behavioral models must be validated against transistor-level results for the effects that matter."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "How should a team keep a behavioral model trustworthy as the transistor-level design changes?",
		A: "Run the same testbench on model and schematic regularly and compare key metrics within tolerances", D: []string{"Write the model once and freeze it", "Trust the model if it compiles", "Compare only DC operating points"},
		Why: "Model-versus-schematic equivalence checks catch drift; this is the analog form of golden testing."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "Why is a pure digital simulation with an analog block stubbed as ideal often insufficient for a calibration loop?",
		A: "Calibration exists to correct analog non-idealities, so an ideal stub gives the loop nothing to correct", D: []string{"Digital simulators cannot simulate loops", "Ideal stubs always crash", "Calibration is purely analog"},
		Why: "The model must include the error being calibrated (offset, gain error, mismatch) for the loop to be exercised."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "What does a mixed-signal simulator gain from a fixed partition of which instances are analog and which digital?",
		A: "Each engine solves only its part, and only boundary nets need connect modules and synchronization", D: []string{"Every net becomes a real number", "Partitioning removes the need for timestep control", "Both engines solve the full circuit for cross-checking"},
		Why: "Partitioning keeps the expensive analog solve small and confines the coupling to boundary nets."},
}

var vaItems = []edakit.Item{
	{KP: 1, Q: "In Verilog-A, what does the statement `I(p, n) <+ V(p, n) / r;` do?",
		A: "Adds a current contribution to branch (p, n), which the simulator stamps into its nodal equations like a resistor", D: []string{"Assigns a new value to node voltage V(p, n)", "Declares a digital wire", "Prints the current every timestep"},
		Why: "The contribution operator adds to a branch quantity; the simulator gathers all contributions into its MNA system."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "Two contributions are made to the same branch: `I(a, b) <+ g*V(a, b);` and `I(a, b) <+ c*ddt(V(a, b));`. What results?",
		A: "A conductance in parallel with a capacitance, because contributions to one branch add", D: []string{"Only the second statement takes effect", "A conductance in series with a capacitance", "A compile error"},
		Why: "Contributions accumulate. Current terms on the same branch sum, which models parallel elements."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "What does `ddt(x)` mean inside a Verilog-A analog block?",
		A: "The time derivative of x, which the simulator discretizes with its integration method", D: []string{"The value of x one timestep earlier", "A delay of x by one clock cycle", "The derivative with respect to temperature"},
		Why: "The simulator turns ddt into a companion model during transient analysis and into j-omega in AC analysis."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "A model contributes a voltage, `V(out) <+ gain*V(in);`. How does a simulator handle a voltage contribution?",
		A: "It adds a branch current unknown, like a voltage source in modified nodal analysis", D: []string{"It converts it into a current through an infinite resistor", "It ignores it during transient analysis", "It requires the node to be grounded"},
		Why: "Voltage-defined branches need an extra current unknown, exactly as voltage sources do in MNA."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "Compact device models such as BSIM-CMG are distributed in Verilog-A. What does a simulator need before it can use them?",
		A: "A compiler that turns the model into code providing currents, charges and their derivatives for Newton iteration", D: []string{"A digital synthesis tool", "A Liberty file", "Nothing, Verilog-A is interpreted by the netlist parser"},
		Why: "Model compilers such as ADMS or OpenVAF generate the evaluation code and Jacobian entries the solver needs."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "Why does a simulator need derivatives of every Verilog-A contribution with respect to the node voltages?",
		A: "Newton-Raphson needs the Jacobian to linearize the nonlinear equations at each iteration", D: []string{"To print sensitivity reports", "To compute the netlist hierarchy", "Only for AC analysis of linear elements"},
		Why: "Each Newton step solves a linear system built from the Jacobian, so the compiler differentiates every contribution automatically."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "A behavioral comparator model uses `if (V(inp) > V(inn)) V(out) <+ vdd; else V(out) <+ 0;`. Transient runs show tiny timesteps and convergence failures. Why?",
		A: "The output jumps discontinuously, so the solver cannot satisfy its error control; a transition filter or smooth function is needed", D: []string{"The if statement is illegal in Verilog-A", "Comparators cannot be modeled behaviorally", "The model has too few parameters"},
		Why: "Discontinuities break the smoothness that Newton and timestep control assume. transition() or tanh-based outputs give finite slopes."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "A model must sample its input exactly when a clock crosses mid-supply. Which construct makes the simulator place a timepoint at that crossing?",
		A: "An @(cross(V(clk) - vdd/2, +1)) event", D: []string{"A for loop over the abstime value", "A ddt of the clock", "An idt of the clock"},
		Why: "cross() events tell the solver to locate the threshold crossing precisely and run the event code there."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "Why is the abstime system function (current simulation time) used carefully in Verilog-A models of sources?",
		A: "Waveforms written directly in terms of abstime need explicit breakpoints, or the simulator may step over sharp features", D: []string{"abstime is always zero in transient analysis", "abstime is only defined in DC analysis", "Using abstime makes a model digital"},
		Why: "The solver chooses timepoints from error estimates; a model with corners in time must announce them, for example with the bound_step system task or events."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "A Verilog-A model divides by V(a, b), which can be zero at the start of a DC solve. What is the robust fix?",
		A: "Guard or regularize the expression so it stays finite and smooth, for example adding a small offset or limiting the denominator", D: []string{"Remove the model from the DC solve", "Use a larger timestep", "Declare the node as digital"},
		Why: "Newton iterations visit unphysical points, so models must stay finite and differentiable everywhere."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "Why are Verilog-A behavioral models preferred over macro-models built from ideal SPICE sources for early system exploration?",
		A: "They express equations directly, including nonlinearities and events, while staying portable across simulators", D: []string{"They always simulate faster than any SPICE netlist", "They need no validation", "They can be synthesized into gates"},
		Why: "Verilog-A is a standard language for analog behaviour; models are concise and portable, but still need validation."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "A Verilog-A model contains internal state updated in an @(timer(...)) event. What must a designer remember about AC analysis of this model?",
		A: "AC analysis linearizes about the DC operating point, so event-driven state changes do not appear in the small-signal response", D: []string{"AC analysis replays every timer event", "AC analysis is illegal for any Verilog-A model", "AC analysis uses the final transient state"},
		Why: "Small-signal analysis sees only the linearization at the operating point."},
}

